[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged on subject lines ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
so every report about a wrong date went into it. Read by tenant, booking form and what people
actually describe, the reports hold three different problems. One fix will not close them all,
so each one now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one known issue. Fixing the parser leaves the other two open.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  issues={{
    parser: 'https://github.com/pablocherryfender/mega-dot-dev/issues/1',
    hint: 'https://github.com/pablocherryfender/mega-dot-dev/issues/2',
    'one-day': 'https://github.com/pablocherryfender/mega-dot-dev/issues/3',
  }}
/>

## The problems

### `parser`: day-first dates read month first

The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the day first, so a
booking for 7 April goes out as 4 July, and 12 October as 10 December. This is the bug #4471
describes, and it hits every tenant through the app, email and the booking-v2 form.

Fix: parse dates in the tenant's locale (`dayFirst()` in `causes.ts` already asks the platform).

### `hint`: booking-v3 tells people to type month first

The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, then reads the date the tenant's way,
day first. People type what the hint says, and the date comes out flipped. Only tenants on
booking-v3 have it, and only through the form. The month/day swap looks the same as `parser`, but
the parser fix does not touch this form.

Fix: print the tenant's own order under the field, as booking-v2 does (`DD/MM/YYYY`).

### `one-day`: dates move by one day, no swap

These reports describe a date that moved by one day: Tuesday 12 May shown as Monday 11 May,
"every booking in June is one day off", a client in Lisbon who got Tuesday while a client in
Toronto got Monday, an invite that arrived before the booking was made. A month/day swap cannot
produce any of these. The desk had already noted them (`desk-notes.json`); the #4471 page counted
them under `parser` as a footnote.

Likely cause, not yet confirmed: a date converted through UTC or the viewer's time zone, so it lands
on the day before. Engineering should check how invite dates are stored and rendered.

## How these were counted

- **By id.** The ticket system exports in pages that overlap, so some reports come twice. Each
  report is counted once.
- **By tenant and form, not by intake tag.** Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser,
  and that guess often disagrees with the tenant (see `causes.ts`). The tag is ignored here.
- **By what people describe.** A month/day swap and a one-day move are different symptoms, so they
  are different problems, even when the subject line is the same.

## Lessons

- A subject line tells you what people noticed, not what broke. Merge on cause, not subject.
- Check a big ticket's reports against the tenant directory before marking it known.

